 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Realising afterwards",
     "texto": [
      "To say that after doing something you discover something, use **-고 보니(까)**: 먹다 → **먹고 보니** (having eaten it, it turned out…), 읽다 → **읽고 보니** (having read it, it turned out…), 사다 → **사고 보니** (having bought it, it turned out…).",
      "이 책을 **읽고 보니** 정말 재미있었어요 = having read this book, it turned out to be really fun."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "With surprise",
     "texto": [
      "The second part almost always expresses what you discovered or a surprise.",
      "사고 보니 너무 비쌌어요 = having bought it, it turned out to be really expensive."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Pretending",
     "texto": [
      "**-는 척하다** is added to the present modifier of a verb to pretend an action, and **-(으)ㄴ 척하다** to the modifier of an adjective. With nouns, **N인 척하다** is used. In the past, use the past modifier: **자는 척했어요** = pretended to sleep; **아픈 척했어요** = pretended to be ill; **학생인 척했어요** = pretended to be a student. The form on its own does not mean that the speaker believes the lie."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Reaction of surprise",
     "texto": [
      "**-다니(요)** expresses surprise, disbelief or a reaction to some information, usually repeating part of what was said: **벌써 끝났다니요!** = What do you mean it's already finished?! It is not the same as **-다고요**, which repeats or confirms a statement with emphasis."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
